{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And you shall command.</b> It is necessary to know why it was necessary to say ״ואתה״ [and you] since He was speaking with him; further, why did He say ״תצוה״ [you shall command] and not ״צו״ [command]? Further, why did He use the language of commandment? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said homiletical interpretations of this: among them, they said (Torat Kohanim, end of parashat Emor) that ״צו״ is nothing but an exhortation, on account of the fact that there is a shortage of money." "Or perhaps, since the matter is not among those entrusted to the commandments of HaShem — that they light lamps in the House of HaShem from which light goes forth — therefore He said to Moses that this commandment is not to be said to Israel by the mouth of HaShem, but rather he himself shall command it — meaning, from his own mouth — that they should conduct this honor of bringing oil to light. And for this He said <b>ואתה</b> [and you], meaning: in addition to what I have commanded you, you add to command them concerning this. And He was precise to say <b>תצוה</b> [you shall command] and not ״תדבר״ [you shall speak], to vindicate our words — that he himself exhorts them concerning the matter, for if He had said ״תדבר״ the implication would be that he speaks the words of HaShem in the usual manner of speaking, without the need for exhortation. And for this reason He did not say ״צו,״ for then it would be understood that He said it because HaShem told him to command; rather, <b>תצוה</b> — meaning, from yourself. And let it not seem in your eyes that this commandment is light since it comes only from the mouth of Moses; that is not so, for this is only because of the way of honor to the Supreme El, but the commandment itself — behold, we see that HaShem did tell him to command it. Or perhaps it is for this that He said <b>ולקחו אליך</b> [and they shall take to you], meaning: for whatever you in your mind command." "Further, it may intend that since HaShem commanded all the work of the Mishkan and its matters face to face, so that it appears He is commanding him to do himself all that is asked of him and to bring all the donations to the Mishkan — given his fervor in the service of HaShem he would also do the work of the Mishkan and its service — therefore He said to him <b>ואתה תצוה</b>, meaning: whatever superiority you have over all Israel is only in what you command them alone. And for this He said the word <b>ואתה</b>, and the addition of the vav is because there are things that Moses does alone. And it is also precise that He said <b>תצוה</b> and did not say ״צו.״" "<b>Further,</b> He hints that since the time had come to give the priesthood to Aaron, HaShem selected the kingship for Moses and crowned him over Israel — and that is His saying <b>תצוה את בני ישראל</b> [you shall command the Children of Israel], in the manner of the verse (1 Samuel 13:14) ״ויצוהו ה׳ לנגיד״ [and HaShem commanded him to be prince], etc. And although in the land of Egypt the verse said (Exodus 6:13) ״ויצום אל בני ישראל״ [and He commanded them concerning the Children of Israel], and we explained there that He made them rulers over the people, here Moses was singled out for the kingship of Israel and Aaron for priesthood. Or perhaps this is what the verse intended when it said (Exodus 19:6) ״ואתם תהיו לי ממלכת כהנים וגוי קדוש״ [and you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation]: ממלכת [kingdom] refers back to Moses; כהנים [priests] — Aaron and his sons; וגוי קדוש [and a holy nation] — these are the Levites." "Or perhaps He hints in His saying <b>תצוה</b>, in the manner of their saying of blessed memory (Zohar 1:83a) that every Torah scholar has a spark from the soul of Moses. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sukkah 39a) would call one another Moses, for he — meaning, this one — speaks in the aspect of the soul of one who studies Torah. And that is His saying <b>ואתה תצוה</b>, in the manner of the verse (Psalms 91:11) ״כי מלאכיו יצוה לך״ [for He will command His angels concerning you] — which is the language of צוותא, accompaniment, for an Israelite who is honored. And you already know that there is no distinction except through engagement in Torah. And HaShem chose to hint this hint in the commandment of the oil, for it is in the aspect of Torah, which is the light of the world. And see the verse that follows this one." "<b>And they shall take to you.</b> The reason for His saying <b>to you</b> will be explained according to what they said in tractate Shabbat 22a, and this is their language: ״outside the veil of the testimony״ — it is a testimony to all who come into the world that the Shekhinah dwells in Israel; what is the testimony? Rav said: this is the western lamp, etc., until here. Now, they said of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 52:2): the scoffers of the generation would mock Moses, saying, is it possible that the Shekhinah rests upon the handiwork of Moses, etc., until here. This shows that they did not concede even among Israel that the Shekhinah would dwell in the Mishkan, and accordingly even though the Shekhinah dwelt on the first of Nisan, none of this is a sign for permanence, that the Shekhinah would be fixed in its place in the Mishkan; but through the miracle of the western lamp there is testimony to all who come into the world that the Shekhinah dwells in Israel. And this is the meaning of the verse <b>and they shall take to you</b> — that is, the matter touches you, for through this your prophecy will be strengthened, that they will recognize and validate your prophecy that the Shekhinah will dwell among them when they see the miracle that will be performed with the oil of the Menorah. And perhaps it was to this that He intended in saying <b>and you shall command</b>, to say that one should not be concerned that his words would not be believed, for the matter would be wondrous — that El Elyon would dwell among the lower ones — and so indeed it was. And if you say, from where would they validate it — <b>and they shall take to you</b>, meaning, in this matter you have reinforcement and validation of the things as mentioned." "<b>And by way of</b> remez, the verse will be explained according to what is brought in Sefer Zohar Hadash (Genesis 8b), that the four exiles of Israel — each one of them Israel was redeemed from it in the merit of one: the first exile they were redeemed in the merit of Abraham our father, peace be upon him; the second in the merit of Isaac; the third in the merit of Jacob; and the fourth depends on the merit of Moses. And therefore the exile has been prolonged, for as long as they are not engaged in Torah and mitzvot, Moses does not wish to redeem a people idle from Torah. And this is what the verse alludes to here in saying <b>and you shall command the children of Israel</b> — after the manner of (Psalms 91:11) ״כִּי מַלְאָכָיו יְצַוֶּה״, or after the manner of their statement of blessed memory: ״צַו״ means nothing but kingship, for he shall reign over us in the future, and a precondition for this is that Israel engage in Torah. And this is what is meant by <b>and they shall take to you pure olive oil</b> — it alludes to the Torah, which is compared to oil: just as oil illuminates the world, so too the Torah; and these are the words of the Zohar that we cited just above." "And Scripture is precise in saying <b>pure</b>, that one must engage in Torah for its own sake without dregs, which are to contention, heaven forbid, or for self-aggrandizement and the like — these are its dregs." "And He further said <b>beaten</b>, meaning that one must engage in Torah and crush one's body and strength, after the manner of the verse (Numbers 19:14) ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת״ (Berakhot 63b), and I have already written on this matter in my Heftzi (Chefetz HaShem, chapter 5) regarding those who weary themselves in it; see there." "And His saying <b>for the lamp</b> is also to be explained according to our way, for in the time of exile the light of the moon, which is called the maor katan, is darkened, and the companion separates from it and the maidservant inherits her mistress. Therefore He commanded that one direct the olive oil to illuminate the lamp and unite it in alignment, and also direct it toward the maor gadol — for you already know their statement of blessed memory (Megillah 29a) on the verse (Deuteronomy 30:3) ״וְשָׁב ה׳״, that it did not say ״וְהֵשִׁיב״, and also their statement of blessed memory (Ta'anit 5a) on the verse ״לֹא אָבוֹא בְּעִיר״, and understand." "And its saying <b>to cause to go up</b> etc. — a perpetual going-up, and this 'perpetual' has no measure, and she shall no more return to darkness, and HaShem shall be to her an everlasting light. Or perhaps it further intends to say <b>for the lamp, to cause to go up a perpetual lamp</b> — for the great Lamp, He will cause the lamp to go up perpetually, in the manner of His saying (Isaiah 62:8) ״נִשְׁבַּע ה׳ וְגוֹ׳ אִם אֶתֵּן אֶת דְּגָנֵךְ״, and you shall be only above."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {10 "The verse allusions here are playfully reinterpreted: 'תָּמִיד' is read as 'עוֹלָם' (everlasting) evoking Isaiah 60:20 ('וְהָיָה ה׳ לְאוֹר עוֹלָם'), and 'לְהַעֲלוֹת' connects to 'וְהָיִיתָ רַק לְמַעְלָה' (Deuteronomy 28:1). The phrase 'לֹא תָשׁוּב לְדַאֲבָה עוֹד' evokes Jeremiah 13:16 ('וְתָקַשֵׁיחַ עֲרָפְּךָ לְמַטָּה... וְקִוִּיתִי לְאוֹר וְיַעַשׂ לְצַלְמָוֶת'). The Isaiah 62:8 citation is applied to the notion of an oath/swearing not to give away."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:39.842393205Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}